Correct the Rust lifetime pattern so a character literal is not consumed.

lizard's Rust reader treats `'Z'` as the lifetime `'Z`, leaving the closing
quote to open a string that runs to the next apostrophe in the file. In
rust/xtask/src/ci/exec.rs that swallows about 3400 lines, so the reader sees
60 of 135 functions and reports a 30-line function as 2631 lines.

A lifetime is never followed by a quote, so requiring that distinguishes the
two forms.

--- a/lizard_languages/rust.py
+++ b/lizard_languages/rust.py
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@
 
     @staticmethod
     def generate_tokens(source_code, addition='', token_class=None):
-        addition = r"|(?:'\w+\b)"  # lifetimes, labels
+        addition = r"|(?:'\w+\b(?!'))"  # lifetimes, labels (not char literals)
         return CodeReader.generate_tokens(source_code, addition, token_class)
 
 
